Repo: https://github.com/heygen-com/hyperframes
Releases: https://github.com/heygen-com/hyperframes/releases

What it does:
Open-source, Apache-2.0, agent-native HTML-to-video framework. An
agent writes ordinary HTML/CSS/JS plus seekable animation; HyperFrames
deterministically renders exact frames to MP4. It includes CLI, core
runtime, Puppeteer/FFmpeg capture engine, producer, Studio, embeddable
player, WebGL transitions and an AWS Lambda surface. HeyGen says it is
used in production internally.

Why it matters versus another avatar model:
This is a credible open deterministic replacement/complement for the
final assembly layer. Generated avatar clips, B-roll, screenshots,
charts, captions, lower thirds, product cards and data visualizations
can all remain independent assets and be composed by an agent. Unlike
asking a video diffusion model to regenerate an entire clip because
text/captions/layout changed, the deterministic layer can make those
edits cheaply.

Inputs -> outputs:
HTML/CSS/JS + media + structured timing -> deterministic rendered video/MP4=
.

Availability/licensing:
Runnable source; Apache-2.0. Node >=3D22 + FFmpeg. Local rendering is
supported; a Modal/serverless template is also present in the HeyGen
org.

Maturity: PRODUCTIONISH.

Exact experiment:
Build one 20-second AGI Everyone vertical short as a HyperFrames
composition. Feed it (1) a reusable avatar A-roll clip, (2) generated
B-roll, (3) dynamic captions, (4) a screenshot/chart, and (5)
TTS/audio. Then ask a coding agent to make 20 hook/layout/caption
variants without regenerating A-roll or B-roll. Measure render time
and marginal cost per variant versus our current Remotion/FFmpeg
approach.

Replace/complement:
Do not replace the character/runtime/Performance IR. Test HyperFrames
against Remotion as the deterministic post-production/compositing
layer. Keep FFmpeg underneath/alongside it.

STACK DELTA
persona/memory -> creative model -> Jev-like cheap policy ->
Performance IR -> cheap avatar draft -> multimodal critic -> selected
photoreal avatar/B-roll assets -> HyperFrames deterministic
agent-authored composition -> publish -> audience metrics.

The key architectural point is that expensive stochastic generation
should create reusable media assets; cheap deterministic HTML/video
composition should create large numbers of final variants.

